  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies in the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add or subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot \\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$",
